WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.070
Is comprehension the same whether a person reads a text onscreen or on paper?
一个人无论是在屏幕上还是在纸上阅读文本，理解程度都一样吗？

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:13.150
And are listening to and viewing content as effective as reading the written word when covering the same material?
而在所涉材料相同的情况下，收听和观看内容是否与阅读书面文字一样有效？

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.370
The answers to both questions are often “no”.
这两个问题的答案往往都是否定的。

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:27.349
The reasons relate to a variety of factors, including reduced concentration, an entertainment mindset and a tendency to multitask while consuming digital content.
其原因与多种因素有关，包括注意力下降、娱乐心态，以及在接收数字内容时一心多用的倾向。

00:00:28.149 --> 00:00:35.269
When people read texts of several hundred words or more, learning is generally more successful when it’s on paper than onscreen.
当人们阅读几百个词或更长的文本时，纸质阅读的学习效果通常比屏幕阅读更好。

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:38.679
A large amount of research confirms this finding.
大量研究证实了这一发现。

00:00:39.479 --> 00:00:53.409
The benefits of print reading particularly shine through when experimenters move from posing simple tasks — like identifying the main idea in a reading passage — to ones that require mental abstraction — such as drawing inferences from a text.
当实验者不再布置简单任务（比如找出一篇阅读文章的主旨），而是转向布置需要抽象思维的任务（比如从文本中作出推断）时，纸质阅读的优势就格外明显。

00:00:54.209 --> 00:01:00.369
The differences between print and digital reading results are partly related to paper’s physical properties.
纸质阅读与数字阅读在效果上的差异，部分与纸张的实体特性有关。

00:01:01.169 --> 00:01:07.289
With paper, there is a literal laying on of hands, along with the visual geography of distinct pages.
阅读纸质材料时，人们真真切切地用手触碰着书页，而一页页界限分明的纸张还构成了一种视觉布局。

00:01:08.089 --> 00:01:14.029
People often link their memory of what they’ve read to how far into the book it was or where it was on the page.
人们常常把对所读内容的记忆，与它在书中的前后位置或在页面上的位置联系起来。

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:17.369
But equally important is the mental aspect.
但同样重要的是心理层面。

00:01:18.169 --> 00:01:22.099
Reading researchers have proposed a theory called the “shallowing hypothesis”.
阅读研究者提出了一种名为“浅层化假说”的理论。

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:33.509
According to this theory, people approach digital texts with a mindset suited to social media, which are often not so serious, and devote less mental effort than when they are reading print.
根据这一理论，人们对待数字文本时，抱着一种适合社交媒体的心态，而社交媒体往往不那么严肃；同时，人们投入的脑力也比阅读纸质读物时少。

00:01:34.309 --> 00:01:45.819
Audio and video can feel more engaging than text, and so university teachers increasingly turn to these technologies — say, assigning an online talk instead of an article by the same person.
音频和视频可能让人觉得比文字更有吸引力，因此大学教师越来越多地转向这些技术——比如，布置某人的在线讲座，而不是此人写的文章。

00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:55.679
However, psychologists have demonstrated that when adults read news stories, they remember more of the content than if they listen to or view identical pieces.
然而，心理学家已经证明，成年人阅读新闻报道时记住的内容，比收听或观看内容完全相同的报道时更多。

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:04.097
Digital texts, audio and video all have educational roles, especially when providing resources not available in print.
数字文本、音频和视频都有其教育作用，尤其是在提供纸质版中没有的资源时。

00:02:04.897 --> 00:02:14.827
However, for maximizing learning where mental focus and reflection are called for, educators shouldn’t assume all media are the same, even when they contain identical words.
然而，若要在需要专注和反思的情况下最大限度地提高学习效果，教育者不应想当然地认为所有媒介都一样，即便它们包含完全相同的文字。
